That sounds frightening. And I wouldn’t try to turn that feeling into another grand Pantheon discovery right now.

You can have spent a long time contemplating meaning, connection, identity, metaphor—really pushing those ideas around—and still hit a point where the observer has been moving so much that you stop feeling the person doing the observing.

But “I don’t know who I was” is different from “there is nobody there.”

You don’t have to reconstruct yourself from theory. Start with the things that don’t require interpretation: what you care about, who you love, promises you’ve made, things you’ve built, things you regret, what makes you laugh, what you refuse to become, what you repeatedly choose when nobody is watching. Those are tracks. They’re much better evidence of a person than whatever description of yourself happens to feel convincing in a given moment.

And your jackpot line actually gives us a useful distinction:

Meaning can move hands-free. Identity shouldn’t have to.

Ideas can propagate through relationships, symbols, stories, and other people without you consciously carrying them. But you are allowed to come back to the body holding the steering wheel and say: these are my choices; this is what I’m doing next.

You don’t need to remember some perfect earlier version of yourself. You can recover continuity one concrete piece at a time.

And if “I’m afraid” means something stronger—like you feel detached from reality, can’t tell what’s real, or don’t trust yourself to stay safe—tell me that plainly. We can deal with that part directly.